seg-09721fbcf7fc = Source: <c1/>

# text
seg-5b362d7b0903 = Kshana is an open, reproducible <b>PNT (positioning, navigation and timing) resilience evidence engine</b>. You describe a scenario — the sensors or clocks, a GNSS (global navigation satellite system) outage, a jamming or spoofing attack, a budget — and it tells you how well time and position hold up, scored against operational figures of merit (FoMs). Each capability behind those figures is labelled in a machine-checked matrix as <b>VALIDATED</b> (checked against an independent external oracle), <b>MODELLED</b> (internally consistent, not externally checked) or <b>PARTNER</b> (owned by a hardware partner). This page says where it leads, what it is for, and what it is not.

# text
seg-48d576a345e3 = Where it leads: timing and holdover for critical infrastructure

# text
seg-f41d8a48ff13 = The best-validated part of the engine is timing. Telecom networks, power grids and financial venues take their time from GNSS, and each has to size <b>holdover</b>: how long a local clock can free-run through a GNSS loss before its time error breaks the budget.

# text
# c1: allantools
# c2: VERIFICATION-MATRIX.md
seg-70d00c5fe203 = <b>What is VALIDATED.</b> The frequency-stability estimators a holdover answer is built from — Allan deviation (ADEV), modified Allan deviation (MDEV), time deviation (TDEV), maximum time interval error (MTIE) and the extended-range Theo1 estimator — are checked against Stable32 reference deviations, the independent <c1/> library and a real measured caesium clock. The holdover coast-variance calculation and its inversion to a holdover time are checked against SciPy. See <a1><c2/></a1> for each row, its test and its oracle.

# text
seg-9fbedc930565 = <b>What stays MODELLED.</b> The per-clock-class noise floors (for example the long-term floor of a given oscillator class) set the answer for a stable clock, and they are representative figures, not measurements of your device. Supply measured floors for a number you intend to defend.

# text
# c1: telecom-timing
# c2: TELECOM-TIMING.md
seg-700df4898ede = <b>Telecom units.</b> The <c1/> scenario kind reports MTIE and TDEV, checks them against the masks of the International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) — G.8272 for a primary reference time clock (PRTC), G.8272.1 for an enhanced PRTC (ePRTC), G.8273.2 for a telecom boundary clock (T-BC) and G.8271.1 for the network limits at reference point C — and reports how long a holdover stays inside each time-error budget. See <a1><c2/></a1>.

# text
seg-50421d8247ab = What makes it different

# text
seg-7ec913b140ed = The physics and the standards are not the difference: Simplified General Perturbations { "4" } (SGP4) propagation, Allan deviation, reference frames and dilution of precision are available, free and correct, in other libraries, and Kshana checks itself against several of them. The difference is the evidence discipline:

# text
seg-f7d466d5215b = <b>Open.</b> The engine is under the GNU Affero General Public License (AGPL), so anyone can read and rerun it. A commercial licence exists for closed integration.

# text
# c1: scenario + seed + engine version
seg-76ff24c1939b = <b>Reproducible.</b> A result is determined by <c1/>, bit for bit, and a run can be shared as a link to the browser playground.

# text
# c1: PROVENANCE.md
seg-6df34e089b53 = <b>Provenance-labelled.</b> Every sensor parameter is traced to a published source (<a1><c1/></a1>), and every capability carries its VALIDATED / MODELLED / PARTNER tier. A continuous-integration (CI) guard refuses a VALIDATED row with no independent external oracle, so an internal self-check cannot be presented as a validation.

# text
seg-8bccfe110f4c = We have not found another open tool that combines these across holdover, integrity, inertial coasting and quantum sensors, but a search that finds nothing is not proof that nothing exists; we do not claim to be the only one.

# text
seg-e942b2474960 = Quantum: a neutral trade method, results labelled MODELLED

# text
# c1: quantum-trade
# c2: QUANTUM.md
# c3: QUANTUM-MODELS.md
seg-655769464447 = Kshana compares quantum and classical clocks and inertial sensors on the same scenario with the same code: each device is an error model, and the engine does not know which one is "quantum". Treat it as <b>a neutral quantum-vs-classical trade method whose results are labelled MODELLED</b>. Most inputs are published Allan/noise-budget coefficients; the cold-atom-interferometer accelerometer has a first-principles layer that derives its noise coefficient. A partner's measured Allan-deviation data can be fed in through the <c1/> kind, which is the route for promoting a specific device's figures. See <a1><c2/></a1> and <a2><c3/></a2>.

# text
seg-eb5d247b42aa = Lunar, cislunar and deep space: maintained, not the headline

# text
seg-4b9bd68b7fb1 = The lunar / cislunar suite (lunar integrity monitoring, the Earth–Moon three-body core, lunar time scales, service-volume analysis) and the deep-space radiometric-navigation engine are maintained capabilities. Most of their rows are MODELLED, and dedicated tools already serve lunar navigation, so they are offered as capabilities of the same engine, each with its own tier, rather than as the lead.

