seg-89d1923b3ed9 = <b>Low Earth orbit (LEO) PNT, end to end.</b> Signal design (<c1/>), pass and per-band link budget (<c2/>), broadcast navigation message (<c3/>), fused medium- and low-Earth-orbit positioning (<c4/>), precise point positioning (PPP) convergence with LEO augmentation (<c5/>), positioning from a 5G non-terrestrial network (<c6/>), and <c7/>, which hands each stage's output to the next for one system.

# text
# c1: spectrum
seg-2e5b57ccd454 = <b>Spectrum.</b> An L-band (and ultra-high-frequency (UHF), S- and C-band) power-spectral-density waterfall under a scripted jammer timeline, with per-band jammer-to-signal ratio and effective carrier-to-noise density (<c1/>), and Signal Metadata Format (SigMF) recording in and out.

# text
# c1: solar-system
# c2: body-pnt
# c3: constellation-design
seg-0e7fd993a838 = <b>The solar system and any central body.</b> Positions and physical constants of the planets and major moons (<c1/>), positioning around any of those bodies (<c2/>), and Walker and published-slot constellation design with coverage and dilution-of-precision maps around any of them (<c3/>).

# text
# c1: campaign
seg-9d993a356eb0 = <b>Campaigns.</b> Existing kinds chained on one shared timeline, swept, run as seeded Monte Carlo ensembles, or run side by side under shared conditions (<c1/>).

# text
# c1: --animate
# c2: --export
# c3: spectrum
seg-905a9aa8c47c = <b>Outputs.</b> Every run writes a result document, a chart and a report in HTML (HyperText Markup Language) and JSON (JavaScript Object Notation); <c1/> writes an SVG (Scalable Vector Graphics), HTML or frame-sequence animation; <c2/> writes CZML (Cesium Language), KML (Keyhole Markup Language), GeoJSON or STK (Systems Tool Kit) ephemeris for the kinds that place things on the Earth, and SigMF for <c3/>.

# text
seg-75206591a52a = Most of these rows are MODELLED; the verification matrix names the few that are VALIDATED (for example the International Telecommunication Union rain and scintillation recommendations, the Galileo broadcast-ephemeris algorithm, the CRC-24Q (24-bit cyclic redundancy check) frame check and the planetary light-time solver).

# text
seg-785218a0ea66 = What Kshana is not

# text
# c1: iq
# c2: spectrum
# c3: [iq]
# c4: spoof_capture
seg-fae54ec38477 = <b>Not a radio-frequency (RF) signal simulator or hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) rig.</b> Kshana drives no radio hardware and transmits nothing. Most of it works at the level of error models and link budgets, before hardware or lab time is bought. At the signal level it is software only: the GNSS IQ layer (<c1/>) writes baseband in-phase/quadrature (IQ) sample files of legitimate GNSS signals for software receivers and runs its own software receiver on them; the <c2/> kind's <c3/> section (since { "0.29.0" }) writes a SigMF (Signal Metadata Format) snapshot of its analytic model that includes the configured jammers (noise-like jammers bin by bin, tones and chirps as waveforms); and <c4/> sums an authentic and a spoofer replica signal in memory to test loop capture, without writing it out. Testing a hardware receiver against real RF stimulus is the job of an RF simulator.

# text
seg-ca243f3f8e8d = <b>Not a replacement for MATLAB/Simulink, STK or Orekit.</b> It sits next to them. It reads and writes the exchange formats they use — CCSDS (Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems) Orbit Ephemeris Messages, SP3 (Standard Product { "3" }) precise orbits, RINEX (Receiver Independent Exchange Format) — writes CZML, KML, GeoJSON and STK ephemeris for their viewers, and its numerical force model is cross-checked against Orekit rather than offered in its place.

# text
seg-8fa4e93c3d0e = <b>Not a carrier-phase GNSS processing engine or positioning service</b> (see below).

# text
seg-8ba4b67bc813 = <b>Not a certification authority.</b> A VALIDATED label means a capability matches an external oracle; it is not a certification of any product or operator.

# text
seg-362b857140b9 = What Kshana is, and is not, next to a GNSS processing engine

# text
# c1: leo-ppp
# c2: pvt
seg-f50951b2e9c8 = Kshana is <b>not</b> a carrier-phase GNSS <em>processing</em> engine, and no scenario kind is a positioning service. It does have a foot in the measurement domain, as library code that the performance study leans on and that is checked against independent references. Short-baseline real-time kinematic (RTK) positioning with LAMBDA (least-squares ambiguity decorrelation adjustment) integer fixing runs on real dual-frequency RINEX observations, as does a static float precise point positioning (PPP) filter with IGS final orbits and clocks, and the receiver clock that filter estimates is a time solution against the IGS final station clock. Each of the three is a VALIDATED row of the <a1>verification matrix</a1>, against the IGS station data it names there, and each runs on static, short observation windows, not as a streaming service with ambiguity resolution over long baselines, carrier smoothing or a kinematic rover. The <c1/> kind runs a float PPP filter on code and carrier phase, but on measurements it simulates itself, to study convergence time with and without LEO augmentation; it does not process an observation file. The <c2/> kind (position, velocity and time) solves a receiver position from real RINEX code pseudoranges plus broadcast ephemeris, validated against a surveyed coordinate of the International GNSS Service (IGS) (station ABMF, 2018-05-13) to <b>{ "5.7" } m 3-D RMS (root mean square) / { "1.1" } m horizontal</b>. But the product is the performance study, not the positioning service, and the framing below is meant to save a prospective user the wrong-tool disappointment.

